  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>To determine the ion(s) present in very small quantity in the solution, we need to consider the chemical reaction that occurs when silver nitrate reacts with potassium iodide. The reaction between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AgNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi></math> is a double replacement reaction and can be represented as follows:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AgNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>KI</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>AgI</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>KNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p>Millimoles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AgNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p><p>Millimoles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mi>KI</mi></math> is in excess &amp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgI</mi></math> forms negatively charged colloid. (Some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>remains in solution) lons <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&amp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>are therefore, present in very small quantity.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
